  "limitations": [
    "Observations come from preview channels only; the live channel is never touched.",
    "Edge-set headers (date, age, via, x-cache*, x-served-by, x-timer, server, alt-svc) are recorded and never compared; any other header that is neither compared nor volatile must be named by the review.",
    "The reserved initialization bodies keep byte count, line count and, for JSON, key order with value types and byte lengths; no value is retained.",
    "ETag and Last-Modified are compared by form, not by value.",
    "The redirect `type` default of the CLI is not observable through the REST API, which requires a status code.",
    "The strict profile matches the recorded cases under productionExact. Under the default profile two development defaults differ from production on purpose (hosting.cacheControl \"none\": no default Cache-Control; hosting.clientCountry \"honoured\": X-Country-Code and the country cookie select i18n content); productionExact restores production for both (owner policy 1396/1403, coordinator ruling 23). A request outside the corpus is answered by the same rules, not by an observation.",
    "Known differences (known-differences.json): gzip and br encoding (KD-COMPRESSION), the 21,376-byte default 404 page body (KD-DEFAULT-404-BODY), the hosted SDK files (KD-SDK-FILES), the init.json layout beyond apiKey and projectId and the init.json and init.js lengths (KD-INIT-KEYS), the length of the hosted /__/auth/handler page (KD-AUTH-HANDLER-PAGE, ruling 20) and the Location host of a parent-directory 302 (KD-SAFETY-ORIGIN-REDIRECT: production answers a 302 to an internal origin host, strict answers the same 302 to the normalized path of this origin; rulings 17 and 21). Strict-transport-security, x-robots-tag and the SDK-row headers are excluded as edge-set. 21 of the 201 rows of each strict comparison carry a known difference and are counted as KNOWN_DIFFERENCE, not MATCH.",
    "Not compared: the body of init.js in the production-vs-local comparison (only init.json has a layout compared), and the stand-in /__/auth/handler page body (HEAD only was recorded).",
    "Inferred, not recorded: the redirect parameter name rule. Strict reads a name as letters and underscore only, so `:id2` is the parameter `id` followed by a literal `2`. The recording shows only that GET /capture/one/two and GET /capture/:id2/two do not match the source /capture/:id/:id2; other readings fit both rows (`:id2` a literal segment; production rejecting or dropping the rule; digit names allowed but repeated parameters refused). The rule applies to every user redirect with a digit in a parameter name (ruling 24); probing digit-bearing names is a To-Do for the next idp packet.",
    "Strict answers that no recording covers: 416 with Content-Range: bytes */N for an unsatisfiable range; a multi-range request answered whole; Range on HEAD answering 206 with the part's length and no body; If-Modified-Since on HEAD; OPTIONS on a path that is not a file (answered as any request); the normalized-path 302 for dot segments other than a leading parent (`/a/../b` answers 302 to `/b`, `/a/./b` to `/a/b`, and a path that ends in a dot segment ends in a slash as RFC 3986 says: `/a/b/..` answers 302 to `/a/`); a dot-segment path with a raw backslash, control character or space, which is not redirected (a browser reads `\\` as `/`, so `Location: /\\evil.example` would leave the origin) and answers 404; the stand-in /__/auth/handler page; and the redirect rule for digit-bearing parameter names above."
  ],
